{
  "image_name": "2aae7c3d6ab2c0e4ca1c882e58c4fd71.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how immune cells interact with tissue structure, especially the extracellular matrix (ECM), in both normal barriers and injury. On the left, a conceptual scheme shows immune cells communicating with structural cells through soluble signals, physical contact, and environmental changes. These interactions influence whether structural elements are produced or broken down.\n\nA second theme highlights transport: structural cells generate matrix components or fragments, and immune cells can carry this material to sites of tissue injury. The examples suggest this can include movement of degraded ECM or deposition of matrix fragments in damaged organs.\n\nThe bottom part emphasizes that immune cells can also act directly on structural elements. They may degrade ECM, for example by releasing enzymes such as MMPs during leukocyte extravasation from blood vessels, or they may promote ECM production. In skin, a neutrophil appears associated with collagen and other ECM proteins, indicating direct contribution to matrix deposition.\n\nThe examples on the right connect these ideas to specific tissues. In the gut, immune-cell activity seems to alter the intestinal environment, promote toxin uptake, and contribute to epithelial damage. Overall, the figure presents immune cells as active regulators of tissue architecture, not just responders to inflammation.",
